[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Edit a file[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mYou’ll start by editing this README file to learn how to edit a file in Bitbucket.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. Click **Source** on the left side.[m
[32m+[m[32m2. Click the README.md link from the list of files.[m
[32m+[m[32m3. Click the **Edit** button.[m
[32m+[m[32m4. Delete the following text: *Delete this line to make a change to the README from Bitbucket.*[m
[32m+[m[32m5. After making your change, click **Commit** and then **Commit** again in the dialog. The commit page will open and you’ll see the change you just made.[m
[32m+[m[32m6. Go back to the **Source** page.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Create a file[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mNext, you’ll add a new file to this repository.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. Click the **New file** button at the top of the **Source** page.[m
[32m+[m[32m2. Give the file a filename of **contributors.txt**.[m
[32m+[m[32m3. Enter your name in the empty file space.[m
[32m+[m[32m4. Click **Commit** and then **Commit** again in the dialog.[m
[32m+[m[32m5. Go back to the **Source** page.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mBefore you move on, go ahead and explore the repository. You've already seen the **Source** page, but check out the **Commits**, **Branches**, and **Settings** pages.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m---[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Clone a repository[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mUse these steps to clone from SourceTree, our client for using the repository command-line free. Cloning allows you to work on your files locally. If you don't yet have SourceTree, [download and install first](https://www.sourcetreeapp.com/). If you prefer to clone from the command line, see [Clone a repository](https://confluence.atlassian.com/x/4whODQ).[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. You’ll see the clone button under the **Source** heading. Click that button.[m
[32m+[m[32m2. Now click **Check out in SourceTree**. You may need to create a SourceTree account or log in.[m
[32m+[m[32m3. When you see the **Clone New** dialog in SourceTree, update the destination path and name if you’d like to and then click **Clone**.[m
[32m+[m[32m4. Open the directory you just created to see your repository’s files.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mNow that you're more familiar with your Bitbucket repository, go ahead and add a new file locally. You can [push your change back to Bitbucket with SourceTree](https://confluence.atlassian.com/x/iqyBMg), or you can [add, commit,](https://confluence.atlassian.com/x/8QhODQ) and [push from the command line](https://confluence.atlassian.com/x/NQ0zDQ).[m
